  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/storage.rs#impl[StoredGraph/Domain]/fn[format_node_content]` is acceptable.\n\n- **Placement and form:** The citation is on an explicit function with a body. Its explanation is in fenced citation commentary, as the built-in requires.\n- **Substance:** The reason is not just difficulty. The function exists only to satisfy the `Domain` trait for the storage-only fixture `StoredGraph`. It deliberately panics, so any call would show that a storage operation touched graph contents. Zero execution is therefore the expected, intended state.\n- **Agreement with the source:**\n  - The body is `panic!(\"storage operations must not format graph contents\")`, which matches the claim that a formatter call would be an unwanted graph access.\n  - The `StoredGraph` test fixture's other graph methods are stubs. `Graph::node_content` and `Fingerprinting::fingerprint` in `storage.rs` take ignored arguments, which matches the claim that the domain does not read or present node contents.\n  - The test `malformed_records_are_storage_errors_and_bad_outputs_are_output_errors` in `storage.rs` uses `StoredGraph`, which is consistent with the claim that it verifies output decoding.\n  - The claim that agent fixtures exercise formatting separately holds. `crates/habeas-sdk/tests/agent.rs#impl[Memory/Domain]/fn[format_node_content]` is a real formatter that delegates to a configurable closure.\n- **Role:** A `cite` is the permitted role for this built-in. The item does not also carry `impl` or `test` references to the same target, so the citation is not redundant.",
